From: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 13:34:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: implement airtime-aware TXQ scheduling and completion budgets

Under heavy traffic, ath11k experiences frequent -ENOMEM errors ("failed to
transmit frame -12") when the hardware TCL ring fills up. This issue is
more commonly observed in VMs with PCIe passthrough but also occurs on bare
metal systems. It is particularly problematic on devices with a single
shared TCL ring where all traffic classes compete for the same 512
descriptor slots.

ath11k_dp_tx_free_txbuf(), reached for the HTT REINJ and INSPECT statuses,
frees the frame with dev_kfree_skb_any(), unlike every other tx completion
path in dp_tx.c, and so does ath11k_dp_free() for the frames in flight when
firmware recovery tears the rings down. This leaks the tx status clone and
idr entry mac80211 keeps for a frame whose socket asked for status, as
commit 29d15589f084 ("wifi: ath11k: Cleanup mac80211 references on failure
during tx_complete") fixed on three sibling paths. With
NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_AQL, set by the next patch, it also keeps the frame's
airtime charge, which ieee80211_restart_hw() does not reset, and an access
category at its limit is never scheduled again.

Without NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_AQL, mac80211 applies no airtime queue limit,
and a saturated download moves every queued frame into the hardware TX
rings. Set the bit. mac80211 estimates each frame's airtime from the rate
ath11k reports through ieee80211_tx_status_ext(), and charges its 4 us
floor while that rate is unset. Only the PPDU stats set it, so single pdev
chips, which take the rate from the tx completion instead, would stay at
the floor; set it there as well.

Nothing shows ar->dp.num_tx_pending, the MSDUs the hardware has not
completed. Print it per radio in soc_dp_stats. Divided into the pending
airtime in a station's aql file, it gives the airtime AQL charges per MSDU.

mac80211 charges the airtime deficit only through
ieee80211_sta_register_airtime(), which ath11k never calls, so with
NL80211_EXT_FEATURE_AIRTIME_FAIRNESS alone every station stays in credit.
Register fes_duration_us from the PPDU stats, which ath11k already adds to
arsta->tx_duration, and set the bit. The TID comes from the ack/BA status
TLV, sent only for a PPDU that drew a response, then from the completion or
rate TLV, then BE. Each user of a downlink MU PPDU is charged the whole
duration, as arsta->tx_duration is; every PPDU on this hardware had one
user. mac80211 takes only local->active_txq_lock[ac] inside the call, which
runs under ar->data_lock and ab->base_lock.

The wake_tx_queue handler serves only the txq mac80211 passes it, so there
is no deficit round robin between stations. Run the round in the driver:
walk the access category's active txqs with ieee80211_next_txq() and drain
each through the TCL ring check until the airtime queue limit binds or the
queue empties. A full ring ends the round, since all txqs of a round share
it. Each round holds a per-category lock, as in ath10k, because mac80211
forbids concurrent rounds on one category and the next patch also starts
rounds from tx completion. The design was proposed for ath11k in [1].

A station at its airtime queue limit gets airtime back only when the
hardware completes its frames, but ath11k starts a round only from
wake_tx_queue, so the backlog waits for the next frame from the stack.
Start a round for each access category, taken from the frame's queue
mapping, that a reaped completion belonged to, whether the hardware or the
firmware released the frame.

mac80211 charges received airtime to the same deficit, as AIRTIME_USE_RX is
set by default, but ath11k reports none. Register each received PPDU's
duration, which ath11k already adds to rx_duration, once against the PPDU's
first TID, or BE when it names none. The TID comes from the PPDU end user
stats TLV, which is added to the default monitor status filter. The call
runs under ab->base_lock, as on the transmit side:
ath11k_mac_op_sta_state() drops the station from its peer under that lock,
and mac80211 frees it after that without an RCU grace period.

ath11k_dp_tx() returns -ENOSPC when the MSDU idr is full and -ENOMEM when
no TCL descriptor is free or a DMA mapping fails; it frees the frame, and
the round then dequeues and frees the next frame of the same flow. End the
round on those errors, leaving the frames behind queued; other errors
concern only the frame. A category whose round ended this way or on a full
TCL ring may have nothing of its own in flight, so mark it and start its
round from the next tx completion on the device.

ath11k_dp_tx_completion_handler() reaps the whole release ring, and
ath11k_dp_service_srng() charges none of it against the NAPI budget, unlike
the receive handlers. Since the handler now ends in a scheduling round, a
full ring gets one round for all of it. Reap at most the budget per poll
and return the count, so a round runs once per budget of completions; NAPI
polls again at once for the rest.

